1
00:00:03,136 --> 00:00:06,070
Before the law, a word about words.

2
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:13,352
Most words carry more than one meaning, and we choose between them without noticing.

3
00:00:13,402 --> 00:00:21,238
If someone tells you a man fell, you do not yet know whether he lost his footing or
lost his life.

4
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:32,907
If someone says a door is open, it may be unlocked, or it may be standing wide.
Usually the sentence around the word tells us which, and we move on.

5
00:00:32,957 --> 00:00:36,564
But sometimes the sentence does not tell us.

6
00:00:36,614 --> 00:00:48,370
Sometimes a word is doing more work than we can see, and the only way to find out is
to follow it: to go where else it is used, and listen to what it means there.

7
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:58,271
That is what this series does. Four separate laws, and in each one a word that could
mean more than one thing.

8
00:00:58,321 --> 00:01:05,348
We will read the law, stop at the word, and follow it through the rest of the Bible
until it settles.

9
00:01:05,398 --> 00:01:12,830
Then we will know what God said about the blind, because we will know what the words
meant to the people who first heard them.

10
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:21,610
One sentence of law. Twenty-four words in English, given at Sinai, more than three
thousand years ago.

11
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:31,890
You must not curse the deaf or place a stumbling block before the blind, but you
shall fear your God. I am the LORD.

12
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:43,390
Brian read that sentence and stopped at one word. And now, Mister Scaredy Cat. Not
Brian's voice, but his words.

13
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:51,462
[Brian] For me, a stumbling block is an annoyance more than anything: something I
would stumble over, but not necessarily fall down.

14
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:56,975
[Brian] I stumble over curbs and parking stops all the time and somehow always seem
to catch myself.

15
00:01:57,025 --> 00:02:01,609
[Brian] I am fortunate to have enough of a sense of balance and spatial awareness
for this to work.

16
00:02:01,659 --> 00:02:08,794
[Brian] Many blind people I know will easily trip over curbs and parking stops,
usually resulting in serious pain of some sort.

17
00:02:08,844 --> 00:02:15,830
[Brian] So is this notion of a stumbling block more like me tripping slightly, or
more like a blind person falling down and seriously hurting something?

18
00:02:17,505 --> 00:02:28,210
That is the question this episode answers. A trip, or a fall. The answer is in the
word itself, and in where else the Bible uses it.

19
00:02:30,386 --> 00:02:40,134
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

20
00:02:40,184 --> 00:02:48,573
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

21
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:59,647
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so. This episode is part of our series
on the Law: what God commanded about the blind.

22
00:02:59,697 --> 00:03:03,518
In these episodes there are no imagined scenes.

23
00:03:03,568 --> 00:03:13,970
Everything you hear is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice,
from the Hebrew words themselves, and from dusty old Brian.

24
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:20,538
First, where we are.

25
00:03:20,588 --> 00:03:28,695
Leviticus chapter nineteen is a chapter of commands about ordinary life, given to
the whole assembly of Israel at Mount Sinai,

26
00:03:28,745 --> 00:03:31,738
in the first year after they came out of Egypt.

27
00:03:31,788 --> 00:03:43,708
The verses around our verse are about leaving gleanings for the poor, paying a hired
man on time, honest courts, and loving your neighbor as yourself.

28
00:03:43,758 --> 00:03:52,040
The law about the blind sits in the middle of them, between wages and justice. Where
this is.

29
00:03:52,090 --> 00:04:03,436
Sinai lies far to the south of everything else in this series, off the bottom edge
of our map. From Jerusalem it is about eleven days' walk, down through the desert.

30
00:04:03,486 --> 00:04:13,809
The second passage we will hear was spoken by Moses on the plains east of the
Jordan, forty years later, and it looks forward to a place inside the land:

31
00:04:13,859 --> 00:04:20,182
two mountains facing each other across a narrow valley, with the town of Shechem
between them.

32
00:04:20,232 --> 00:04:25,668
Stand in Jerusalem and face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

33
00:04:25,718 --> 00:04:35,541
Shechem is at twelve o'clock, about a day and a half's walk, in the hill country
that would become Samaria. Hear the law in its place.

34
00:04:35,591 --> 00:04:39,170
Leviticus chapter 19, verses 13 to 18.

35
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:50,044
[Reader] You must not defraud your neighbor or rob him. You must not withhold until
morning the wages due a hired hand.

36
00:04:50,094 --> 00:04:59,261
[Reader] You must not curse the deaf or place a stumbling block before the blind,
but you shall fear your God. I am the LORD.

37
00:04:59,311 --> 00:05:09,802
[Reader] You must not pervert justice; you must not show partiality to the poor or
favoritism to the rich; you are to judge your neighbor fairly.

38
00:05:09,852 --> 00:05:18,019
[Reader] You must not go about spreading slander among your people. You must not
endanger the life of your neighbor. I am the LORD.

39
00:05:18,069 --> 00:05:26,938
[Reader] You must not harbor hatred against your brother in your heart. Directly
rebuke your neighbor, so that you will not incur guilt on account of him.

40
00:05:26,988 --> 00:05:35,810
[Reader] Do not seek revenge or bear a grudge against any of your people, but love
your neighbor as yourself. I am the LORD.

41
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:55,174
Notice the company it keeps. Wages. Fair courts. Slander. Grudges. Love your
neighbor. The blind man and the deaf man are not set apart in a chapter of their
own.

42
00:05:55,224 --> 00:06:07,193
They are in the chapter about how to treat the people next to you. Now the second
passage. Moses is commanding a ceremony for the day Israel crosses the Jordan.

43
00:06:07,243 --> 00:06:19,512
Six tribes are to stand on one mountain and six on the other, and the Levites are to
call out twelve curses, and the whole nation is to answer each one.

44
00:06:19,562 --> 00:06:27,230
The blind man is in the list. Deuteronomy chapter 27, verses 11 to 19.

45
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:41,407
[Reader] On that day Moses commanded the people: “When you have crossed the Jordan,
these tribes shall stand on Mount Gerizim to bless the people: Simeon, Levi, Judah,

46
00:06:41,457 --> 00:06:43,992
[Reader] Issachar, Joseph, and Benjamin.

47
00:06:44,042 --> 00:06:53,573
[Reader] And these tribes shall stand on Mount Ebal to deliver the curse: Reuben,
Gad, Asher, Zebulun, Dan, and Naphtali.

48
00:06:53,623 --> 00:07:05,293
[Reader] Then the Levites shall proclaim in a loud voice to every Israelite: ‘Cursed
is the man who makes a carved idol or molten image—an abomination to the LORD,

49
00:07:05,343 --> 00:07:10,087
[Reader] the work of the hands of a craftsman—and sets it up in secret.’

50
00:07:10,137 --> 00:07:20,989
[Reader] And let all the people say, ‘Amen!’ ‘Cursed is he who dishonors his father
or mother.’ And let all the people say, ‘Amen!’

51
00:07:21,039 --> 00:07:32,523
[Reader] ‘Cursed is he who moves his neighbor’s boundary stone.’ And let all the
people say, ‘Amen!’ ‘Cursed is he who lets a blind man wander in the road.’

52
00:07:32,573 --> 00:07:45,590
[Reader] And let all the people say, ‘Amen!’ ‘Cursed is he who withholds justice
from the foreigner, the fatherless, or the widow.’ And let all the people say,
‘Amen!’

53
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:50,276
Think about the sound of that.

54
00:07:50,326 --> 00:08:01,846
A valley with a mountain on either side, and on the slopes, a whole people: six
hundred thousand men by the census taken a few weeks before,

55
00:08:01,896 --> 00:08:04,490
with their families beside them.

56
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:13,427
The Levites call out, and the answer comes back from both sides at once. Amen.
Twelve times.

57
00:08:13,477 --> 00:08:18,614
And one of the twelve is for the man who lets a blind traveller wander off the road.

58
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:29,653
Look at who his neighbours are in that list: the man who makes an idol in secret,
the man who moves a boundary stone, the man who cheats the foreigner,

59
00:08:29,703 --> 00:08:31,773
the fatherless and the widow.

60
00:08:31,823 --> 00:08:36,810
Hidden wrongs, every one. Wrongs that no court would ever hear about.

61
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:51,908
Now the word. The Hebrew behind stumbling block is mikshol. Say it with me:
mik-shole.

62
00:08:51,958 --> 00:09:04,376
Strong's dictionary, from 1894, gives it as a stumbling-block, literally or
figuratively, and derives it from a verb that means to totter or fall.

63
00:09:04,426 --> 00:09:15,891
The 1915 encyclopedia says its literal sense is an object which causes one to
stumble or fall, and it says something else that matters for Brian's question.

64
00:09:15,941 --> 00:09:24,284
Leviticus nineteen fourteen, it says, is the one place in the Bible where the word
is plainly literal.

65
00:09:24,334 --> 00:09:37,918
Everywhere else it is used for what brings material ruin or spiritual downfall. So
let us go everywhere else. Here is the same word, mikshol, in four other places.

66
00:09:37,968 --> 00:09:43,630
Listen to what happens to the people in them. Jeremiah chapter 6, verse 21.

67
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:58,010
[Reader] Therefore this is what the LORD says: “I will lay stumbling blocks before
this people; fathers and sons alike will be staggered; friends and neighbors will
perish.”

68
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:03,630
Friends and neighbors will perish. Ezekiel chapter 3, verse 20.

69
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:13,865
[Reader] Now if a righteous man turns from his righteousness and commits iniquity,
and I put a stumbling block before him, he will die.

70
00:10:13,915 --> 00:10:24,850
[Reader] If you did not warn him, he will die in his sin, and the righteous acts he
did will not be remembered. And I will hold you responsible for his blood.

71
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:30,990
He will die. Isaiah chapter 8, verses 14 to 15.

72
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:45,076
[Reader] And He will be a sanctuary—but to both houses of Israel a stone of
stumbling and a rock of offense, to the dwellers of Jerusalem a trap and a snare.

73
00:10:45,126 --> 00:10:52,450
[Reader] Many will stumble over these; they will fall and be broken; they will be
ensnared and captured.”

74
00:10:53,500 --> 00:11:01,130
They will fall and be broken. Psalm chapter 119, verse 165.

75
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:07,970
[Reader] Abundant peace belongs to those who love Your law; nothing can make them
stumble.

76
00:11:08,770 --> 00:11:23,420
Only the psalm is gentle, and there the stumbling block is the thing that cannot
happen to those who love God's law. Perish. Die. Fall and be broken.

77
00:11:23,470 --> 00:11:35,046
That is the company this word keeps. So here is the answer to Brian's question, on
the words alone. The law is about the fall, not the trip.

78
00:11:35,096 --> 00:11:41,920
And in the word's other uses, about worse than a fall. It was written for the people
who do not catch themselves.

79
00:11:45,470 --> 00:11:55,854
Two more words from the same verse. The blind man is ivver, the plain word for
blind, the one you will hear in all four of these episodes.

80
00:11:55,904 --> 00:12:09,305
And the reason the law gives, you shall fear your God, uses yare. Yah-ray. To fear,
or to revere. Hold on to that word.

81
00:12:09,355 --> 00:12:19,679
It opens the Law's care for the blind here, and in the fourth episode you will hear
it close the Law's last word on the subject, a thousand years later.

82
00:12:19,729 --> 00:12:31,920
And the curse in Deuteronomy uses a different verb for what is done to the blind
man. He is made to wander: shagah, to stray, or to be led astray.

83
00:12:31,970 --> 00:12:34,380
The word for going wrong without knowing it.

84
00:12:36,430 --> 00:12:47,310
Now the old books. In these episodes they speak for themselves, in voices of their
own, and I will tell you who is speaking and when.

85
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:54,140
First, Matthew Henry, a pastor writing in England in seventeen hundred and six, on
this verse.

86
00:12:55,190 --> 00:13:04,597
[Matthew Henry] The safety of the blind we must likewise be tender of, and not put a
stumbling-block before them; for this is to add affliction to the afflicted,

87
00:13:04,647 --> 00:13:08,054
[Matthew Henry] and to make God's providence a servant to our malice.

88
00:13:08,104 --> 00:13:13,780
[Matthew Henry] This prohibition implies a precept to help the blind, and remove
stumbling-blocks out of their way.

89
00:13:14,830 --> 00:13:21,020
And on the words, you shall fear your God, Henry gives the reason that still holds.

90
00:13:22,070 --> 00:13:31,460
[Matthew Henry] Thou dost not fear the deaf and blind, they cannot right themselves;
but remember it is the glory of God to help the helpless, and he will plead their
cause.

91
00:13:32,510 --> 00:13:42,331
That is the heart of it. Both wrongs in this verse are wrongs the victim cannot
catch. The deaf man does not hear the curse.

92
00:13:42,381 --> 00:13:55,645
The blind man does not see who moved the stone, or who stood and watched him take
the wrong road. No witness is coming forward. So the law does not appeal to a court.

93
00:13:55,695 --> 00:14:07,580
It appeals to the one who saw. Now Josephus, a Jewish historian writing for Roman
readers about the year ninety-three, retelling the Law in everyday terms.

94
00:14:08,630 --> 00:14:18,827
[The old books] It is also a duty to show the roads to those who do not know them,
and not to esteem it a matter for sport, when we hinder others' advantages,

95
00:14:18,877 --> 00:14:21,064
[The old books] by setting them in a wrong way.

96
00:14:21,114 --> 00:14:25,380
[The old books] In like manner, let no one revile a person blind or dumb.

97
00:14:26,430 --> 00:14:40,342
Three things in that are his, not Leviticus's. The law only forbids; Josephus adds a
duty, to show the way to anyone who does not know it.

98
00:14:40,392 --> 00:14:50,631
The law gives no motive; Josephus names one. Sport. The pleasure of watching
somebody go wrong.

99
00:14:50,681 --> 00:15:03,512
And he pairs the blind with the dumb, those who cannot speak, as Leviticus pairs the
blind with the deaf: people who cannot catch you, and cannot answer back.

100
00:15:03,562 --> 00:15:11,277
So this is what a Jewish writer in the first century thought the law was guarding
against. Not an accident on the road.

101
00:15:11,327 --> 00:15:16,140
A cruel joke, played on someone who would never know who played it.

102
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:26,840
One honest counterweight, from Henry again. He reports that the Jewish commentators
before him found the literal act unthinkable.

103
00:15:27,390 --> 00:15:36,368
[Matthew Henry] The Jewish writers, thinking it impossible that any should be so
barbarous as to put a stumbling-block in the way of the blind, understood it
figuratively,

104
00:15:36,418 --> 00:15:41,260
[Matthew Henry] that it forbids giving bad counsel to those that are simple and
easily imposed upon.

105
00:15:42,810 --> 00:15:54,280
Both readings have been held for a very long time, and they do not have to compete.
The rabbis who read it as bad counsel did not deny the stone in the road.

106
00:15:54,330 --> 00:16:00,920
They thought no one would do it. Henry kept the stone, and he kept the warning about
counsel too.

107
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:12,125
Here is something worth holding on to as we go through these four laws. A law is not
proof that a thing was common.

108
00:16:12,175 --> 00:16:27,505
Nobody passes a law against what nobody does, but nobody passes one against what
everybody does, either. A law is a sign that someone thought a thing worth
forbidding.

109
00:16:27,555 --> 00:16:34,160
Someone looked at what people might do to a blind man, and decided it needed to be
written down.

110
00:16:35,210 --> 00:16:46,863
And here, more than written down. Spoken from a mountain, by the Levites, and
answered by a whole nation. Amen.

111
00:16:46,913 --> 00:16:53,880
Of all the things Israel could have been asked to curse aloud on its first day in
the land, this was one of the twelve.

112
00:16:55,569 --> 00:16:59,300
Brian had an earlier question too, and it is a good one.

113
00:17:00,350 --> 00:17:08,705
[Brian] It doesn't look like Hammurabi's code has anything about the treatment of
the blind. So is the Bible the first recorded anything that addresses the blind?

114
00:17:08,755 --> 00:17:13,740
[Brian] And when was Hammurabi's code written compared with what Moses was doing in
Leviticus and Deuteronomy?

115
00:17:14,790 --> 00:17:22,561
Hammurabi was king of Babylon from about seventeen ninety-two to seventeen fifty
before Christ.

116
00:17:22,611 --> 00:17:34,086
For Moses there are two main datings, one around fourteen ninety and one around
twelve fifty, so Hammurabi comes first by three to five centuries.

117
00:17:34,136 --> 00:17:45,940
His code is the most famous law code of the ancient world, and its prologue makes a
large claim. Here is the king himself, as the 1915 encyclopedia renders him.

118
00:17:46,990 --> 00:17:53,100
[The old books] As a helper of the oppressed, as an adviser of widows and orphans,
in short, as the father of his people.

119
00:17:54,271 --> 00:18:07,606
But when you look for the blind in his two hundred and eighty-two laws, you find
eyes only as injuries to be priced. We will hear those laws in the next episode.

120
00:18:07,656 --> 00:18:11,826
There is no law in Hammurabi protecting a blind person.

121
00:18:11,876 --> 00:18:24,847
Nor in the older code of Ur-Nammu, three centuries before him, which prices a
knocked-out eye at half a mina of silver and says nothing else. So is the Bible
first?

122
00:18:24,897 --> 00:18:37,488
Not quite. One text from Egypt does show regard, the Instruction of Amenemope, dated
somewhere between thirteen hundred and ten seventy-five before Christ.

123
00:18:37,538 --> 00:18:41,140
In Budge's translation of 1924 it reads:

124
00:18:41,490 --> 00:18:50,000
[The old books] Make not a laughing-stock of the blind man. Vex not the dwarf. And
frustrate not the plans of the afflicted.

125
00:18:51,050 --> 00:19:05,170
Notice what Amenemope forbids. Laughter. The same thing Josephus heard in the law of
Moses, sport at a blind man's expense, was worth a warning on the Nile too.

126
00:19:05,220 --> 00:19:14,052
But it is advice from a wise man to his son. It is not law, and it carries no curse
and no God who sees.

127
00:19:14,102 --> 00:19:25,000
As far as the shelf can tell, Leviticus nineteen fourteen is the first law in the
ancient world written to protect a blind person, rather than to price his eye.

128
00:19:26,550 --> 00:19:40,474
Let me put the two laws together and reason it through, with no story and nobody
invented. Suppose a stone is moved into a path a blind man walks every day. He
falls.

129
00:19:40,524 --> 00:19:52,550
Who saw it? Perhaps nobody. Perhaps one man, who is not going to speak. The blind
man cannot say who did it. There is no case.

130
00:19:52,600 --> 00:20:03,203
That is exactly why the first law says, you shall fear your God. It names the only
witness there is.

131
00:20:03,253 --> 00:20:14,510
And suppose a traveller asks the way, and is pointed wrong, for sport, and walks an
hour into the wrong valley. Who would ever prove it? Nobody.

132
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:18,302
That is why the second law is not a fine.

133
00:20:18,352 --> 00:20:30,877
It is a curse, said by the whole nation at once, so that every man in Israel had
stood on a mountainside and said Amen to it before he was ever tempted.

134
00:20:30,927 --> 00:20:41,460
The Law did what law can do for a wrong that leaves no witness. It made the whole
people the witness, and God the judge.

135
00:20:43,810 --> 00:20:45,760
Brian, with the last word.

136
00:20:46,443 --> 00:20:53,857
[Brian] When I started studying this episode, my first thought was that this law did
not mean much to me, because of what I took a stumbling block to be:

137
00:20:53,907 --> 00:20:56,070
[Brian] something you trip over and catch yourself.

138
00:20:56,120 --> 00:21:04,076
[Brian] But the further I read, the more I found comfort where I had not expected
it. I feel the gravity of what God was telling His people so long ago.

139
00:21:04,126 --> 00:21:07,940
[Brian] He is always watching, even when nobody else is, and He knows.

140
00:21:12,699 --> 00:21:25,000
Next time, the one law in the Bible that puts a price on an eye, and what the price
turns out to be. The servant blinded by his master.

141
00:21:27,810 --> 00:21:36,260
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

142
00:21:36,310 --> 00:21:45,685
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

143
00:21:45,735 --> 00:21:52,960
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

